import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';
import { firstRun, evalDefinition, agentDefinition, fixtureDefinition, scorerDefinition, resultsCommand, projectConfig, matrixCommands } from '../../content/snippets';
---

<Site title="Get started — author evals as code with Evalkit" description="Write agent evals in TypeScript, reuse fixtures and scoring rules, compare model settings, and inspect local results." active="docs">
  <div class="docs-header wrap"><span class="section-kicker">EVALKIT / FIELD GUIDE</span><h1>Get started<span class="text-accent">.</span></h1><p>An eval tells Evalkit what task to give your agent, which agent to run, and how to score what happened. Write one as code, reuse its fixtures and scorers, then compare runs or model settings. This guide follows the current local Bun implementation.</p></div>
  <div class="docs-layout wrap">
    <aside class="docs-sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#first-run">01 / First run</a><a href="#create-an-eval">02 / Define an eval</a><a href="#agents">03 / Add an agent</a><a href="#fixtures">04 / Add fixtures</a><a href="#scoring">05 / Score a trial</a><a href="#results">06 / Read results</a><a href="#configure">07 / Configure</a><a href="#matrices">08 / Sweep parameters</a><a href="#next">09 / What’s next</a><div class="sidebar-separator"></div><a href="/docs/manual/">Full manual ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit/tree/main/examples/starter">Starter source ↗</a></aside>
    <div class="docs-body">
      <section id="first-run" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>01 / FIRST RUN</span><h2>Try the starter.</h2></div><p>Evalkit is currently a Bun monorepo. <code>bun create</code> copies the repository into <code>evalkit/</code> and installs its workspace dependencies. Run the self-contained greeting eval first; it shows how a versionable TypeScript definition produces a local, inspectable report without Pi or a model provider.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">TERMINAL</div><Code code={firstRun} lang="bash" theme="github-light" /></div><p>The final command starts the dashboard and prints its local URL. Run it in a second terminal if you want to keep using the first one. <strong>Run commands from your eval project directory:</strong> that’s where Evalkit loads a config or discovers eval files, resolves fixtures, and writes results. The starter also includes an explicit suite registry.</p><div class="callout"><span>NOTE</span><p><code>bun run evals</code> runs the entire starter suite, including Pi-backed evals. Those require a working <code>pi</code> command and configured model access.</p></div><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#run-from-the-cli">Running from the CLI →</a></p></section>

      <section id="create-an-eval" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>02 / DEFINE AN EVAL</span><h2>Describe the task.</h2></div><p>An eval is a TypeScript definition that composes the system being tested (its AUT adapter), user messages, reusable fixtures, and scoring rules. Keep it in Git with your project so teammates can review changes. Give it a stable, human-readable <code>id</code> such as <code>greeting</code>. Use lowercase kebab-case IDs for evals, suites, agents, and matrices; fixtures need no IDs. Evalkit generates run and trial identities.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">evals/greeting.eval.ts</div><Code code={evalDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Place this file under <code>evals/</code> and default-export the eval, as shown. The CLI discovers matching files automatically and runs one with <code>run-evals greeting</code>; no registry or config file is required for this. The starter keeps an explicit registry to demonstrate suites, but that is an optional, more advanced path.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#project-structure-and-registration">Project structure and registration →</a> · <a href="/docs/manual/#add-a-scorer-and-an-eval">Eval definitions →</a></p></section>

      <section id="agents" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>03 / ADD AN AGENT</span><h2>Connect the system you’re testing.</h2></div><p>The <strong>Agent Under Test (AUT)</strong> is the assistant, service, or local program whose behavior you want to measure—not the judge that grades it. An adapter connects that system to Evalkit: for each trial, Evalkit starts a fresh session, sends the eval’s user messages, and records the events the adapter emits. Scorers inspect that trajectory and the resulting files to decide whether the trial passed.</p><p>The adapter implements <code>start()</code>, returning a session with <code>send(message)</code> and <code>close()</code>. <code>send()</code> should resolve when that turn is finished; its return value is not the recorded answer. Emit assistant messages as events so they appear in the timeline and can be scored.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">agents/greeting-agent.ts · essentials</div><Code code={agentDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Use <code>context.workspace.root</code> for candidate-visible files. Never pass <code>context.evaluatorWorkspace.root</code> to the model. For a local Pi process, <code>piAgent()</code> is available from <code>@evalkit/agents</code>, but invokes Pi independently for each message.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#add-an-agent">Adding an agent →</a></p></section>

      <section id="fixtures" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>04 / ADD FIXTURES</span><h2>Give it something to work on.</h2></div><p>Fixtures are ordinary project files or generated inputs you can reuse across eval definitions. They are copied into isolated workspaces before each trial: candidate files are visible to the agent, while evaluator files stay private to trusted scoring code. Source paths resolve from the project directory.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">evals/greeting.eval.ts · fixture options</div><Code code={fixtureDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Add <code>fixtures</code> to the eval definition. The <code>dynamic()</code> helper can generate per-trial fixtures using the trial context and its random seed. No fixture ID is needed: destinations only need to be unique within each trial workspace. Keep expected answers out of candidate files.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#add-fixtures">Fixture types and visibility →</a></p></section>

      <section id="scoring" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>05 / SCORE A TRIAL</span><h2>Make success explicit.</h2></div><p>A reusable predicate scorer is an executable judge you can import into many evals. It examines events and files after the session closes. Return a score between 0 and 1. A numeric score passes only when it is exactly 1; return an object with <code>passed</code> to set your own threshold.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">judges/greeting.ts</div><Code code={scorerDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /></div><p>The callback also receives <code>artifacts.candidate.root</code> and <code>artifacts.evaluator.root</code> to inspect trial files. Score errors are saved, not silently treated as passes.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#add-a-scorer-and-an-eval">Scorers and evals →</a></p></section>

      <section id="results" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>06 / READ RESULTS</span><h2>Follow the evidence.</h2></div><p>Keep eval definitions in Git; results are local files you can compare and review. The starter ignores report directories in Git, so commit only selected, reviewed summaries if your team wants a versioned history of outcomes. Each run lives under <code>_evalkit-results/&lt;run-id&gt;/</code>. Read <code>summary.json</code> for passed and failed trial counts; inside <code>trials/&lt;trial-id&gt;/</code>, inspect <code>trajectory.jsonl</code>, <code>scoring.json</code>, <code>summary.json</code>, and the candidate artifact snapshot.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">TERMINAL · replace the run ID</div><Code code={resultsCommand} lang="bash" theme="github-light" /></div><div class="callout"><span>IMPORTANT</span><p>Execution status <code>completed</code> does not mean the scores passed. For a CI gate, inspect the run-level <code>failed</code> count; the run summary provides detailed trial-level outcomes.</p></div><p>Local <code>_evalkit-sandbox/</code> directories retain candidate <em>and evaluator</em> files. Review them for sensitive data before sharing. The dashboard lets you navigate runs, trial timelines, and candidate files without digging through JSON.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#read-and-process-results">Reading and processing results →</a></p></section>

      <section id="configure" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>07 / CONFIGURE A PROJECT</span><h2>Discover evals by ID.</h2></div><p>Default-export evals from <code>evals/*.eval.ts</code> as in step 02; discovery works without a config file. Add <code>evalkit.config.js</code> only when you want to customize discovery, execution limits, or a model matrix. Evalkit imports matching files in sorted order:</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">evalkit.config.js</div><Code code={projectConfig} lang="js" theme="github-light" /></div><p>Paths, including fixtures and report directories, resolve relative to the config. You can customize <code>include</code>, <code>exclude</code>, and <code>testDir</code>. Run from that project directory. If you need suites, supply an explicit <code>registry</code> in the config or use a <code>src/registry.ts</code> without a config, as the starter does.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#configuration-and-discovery">Project configuration and discovery →</a></p></section>

      <section id="matrices" class="doc-section"><div class="doc-heading"><span>08 / SWEEP PARAMETERS</span><h2>Test more than one setting.</h2></div><p>Run the same eval against many model settings without duplicating its code. A matrix lazily expands evals × parameter axes into independent cells; <code>--eval</code> selects a human eval ID and <code>--model</code> narrows an axis. Inspect the count with <code>--dry-run</code> before running. Plans above <code>execution.maxCells</code> require <code>--all</code>.</p><div class="snippet"><div class="snippet-top">TERMINAL · from the configured project</div><Code code={matrixCommands} lang="bash" theme="github-light" /></div><p>The adapter must read <code>context.parameters.model</code> to apply the selected model; the included <code>piAgent()</code> does not do this automatically. Cell keys and effective parameters are saved with the reports.</p><p class="further-reading">More information: <a href="/docs/manual/#run-from-the-cli">Matrices, filters, and safety limits →</a></p></section>

      <section id="next" class="doc-section doc-last"><div class="doc-heading"><span>09 / WHAT’S NEXT</span><h2>Go further.</h2></div><p>Read the <a href="/docs/manual/">full manual ↗</a> for team-friendly suites, configurable matrices, reusable fixtures and predicates, dashboard controls, and result processing. The code in <a href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit/tree/main/examples/starter">examples/starter ↗</a> is a runnable reference.</p><p>Today, only <code>user(...)</code> transcript steps and <code>predicate(...)</code> scorers execute. Judge scorers, remote Agents SDK transport, enforced timeouts, and project scaffolding are still in progress. Evalkit’s packages are workspace-local for now.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit">Explore the repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Site>
